1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,350
Our skill says each entry should be
written for people who use the tool.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:08,947
Yet when the fix was a switch to a
streaming parser, one model's entry told

3
00:00:08,947 --> 00:00:10,750
users about the streaming parser.

4
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:14,975
None of our graders checks for that,
because no pattern can.

5
00:00:15,475 --> 00:00:19,894
Here, you'll write a grader that asks
another model one yes-or-no question, run

6
00:00:19,894 --> 00:00:22,300
it over saved trials, and read its
reasons.

7
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,350
Let's start with why code can't check
this.

8
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:31,716
Every grader so far is code: a pattern, an
empty-diff check, a script, or a check of

9
00:00:31,716 --> 00:00:32,875
which skills loaded.

10
00:00:33,375 --> 00:00:36,125
Each one checks something code can see.

11
00:00:36,625 --> 00:00:40,731
Whether an entry says how a fix was coded
isn't something a pattern can match,

12
00:00:40,731 --> 00:00:43,100
because the giveaway words could be
anything.

13
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:48,318
Here they're streaming parser, when all a
user cares about is that big imports no

14
00:00:48,318 --> 00:00:49,075
longer crash.

15
00:00:49,575 --> 00:00:53,950
Checking that takes something that can
read, and that something is another model.

16
00:00:55,167 --> 00:01:00,325
That kind of grader is called an LLM
judge, where LLM stands for large language

17
00:01:00,325 --> 00:01:00,717
model.

18
00:01:01,217 --> 00:01:05,799
It asks a language model to read what the
agent did, and decide whether it meets a

19
00:01:05,799 --> 00:01:06,917
criterion you wrote.

20
00:01:07,417 --> 00:01:11,167
Such a judge can decide things code can't,
but it has costs.

21
00:01:11,667 --> 00:01:16,092
It's slower than code, and every trial it
judges costs a call to a model.

22
00:01:16,592 --> 00:01:20,760
It can also be wrong in ways a pattern
can't: swayed by confident wording, or

23
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:23,142
reading your criterion differently from
you.

24
00:01:23,642 --> 00:01:28,809
So we use a judge only for what code can't
decide, and everything graded so far stays

25
00:01:28,809 --> 00:01:29,417
with code.

26
00:01:30,633 --> 00:01:34,308
When a judge is needed, three habits make
its verdicts usable.

27
00:01:34,808 --> 00:01:37,208
First, one question per judge.

28
00:01:37,708 --> 00:01:41,716
Asked whether an entry is good, a judge
weighs several things at once, and you

29
00:01:41,716 --> 00:01:43,308
can't tell which one tipped it.

30
00:01:43,808 --> 00:01:46,433
Second, the answer is yes or no.

31
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:51,161
With a score from one to five, it's hard
for anyone, the judge included, to say

32
00:01:51,161 --> 00:01:53,033
what separates a three from a four.

33
00:01:53,533 --> 00:01:56,783
Yes or no forces the criterion to be
clear.

34
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,850
Third, we decide our own answers first.

35
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:05,919
Before writing the criterion, we go
through real examples, and say yes or no

36
00:02:05,919 --> 00:02:06,400
to each.

37
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:11,029
If we hesitate over one, the criterion
isn't clear yet, and the judge would

38
00:02:11,029 --> 00:02:12,075
inherit the muddle.

39
00:02:12,575 --> 00:02:16,751
Each answer we write down is called a
label, and our labels are what the judge

40
00:02:16,751 --> 00:02:17,875
gets checked against.

41
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:22,650
That's when to use one; here's how Vally's
judge is written.

42
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:27,413
This eval file ships in the practice
folder, and we're looking at it with a

43
00:02:27,413 --> 00:02:28,550
judge already added.

44
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:31,925
It takes three additions, in three places.

45
00:02:32,425 --> 00:02:36,650
First, under defaults, judge model names
the model that does the judging.

46
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:41,599
Without that line Vally picks one, and its
choice can change from one version of

47
00:02:41,599 --> 00:02:42,600
Vally to the next.

48
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:47,831
We name a model that isn't one of the two
being tested, so that no model marks its

49
00:02:47,831 --> 00:02:48,350
own work.

50
00:02:49,567 --> 00:02:54,077
Each stimulus in this file is a request
worded in coding terms, like this one

51
00:02:54,077 --> 00:02:55,542
about a streaming parser.

52
00:02:56,042 --> 00:03:01,046
The second addition is the rubric: a list
of criteria for this stimulus, in plain

53
00:03:01,046 --> 00:03:01,417
words.

54
00:03:01,917 --> 00:03:04,417
Ours has a single criterion.

55
00:03:04,917 --> 00:03:09,021
It asks that the new entry says what
changes for people who use the tool, and

56
00:03:09,021 --> 00:03:10,567
doesn't say how it was coded.

57
00:03:11,767 --> 00:03:15,842
The third addition is the judge itself: a
grader of type prompt.

58
00:03:16,342 --> 00:03:20,765
Its name is unlucky, because it's
unrelated to the prompt field that holds

59
00:03:20,765 --> 00:03:21,542
each request.

60
00:03:22,042 --> 00:03:25,792
This grader reads the rubric of the
stimulus it sits in.

61
00:03:26,292 --> 00:03:29,517
Scoring binary makes its answer yes or no.

62
00:03:30,017 --> 00:03:34,660
On the next line is an extra instruction,
telling the judge to start each reason by

63
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:35,667
quoting the entry.

64
00:03:36,167 --> 00:03:39,717
That way, each reason shows what the judge
was looking at.

65
00:03:40,217 --> 00:03:44,619
Below it is the grader that was there
already, a pattern that checks the entry

66
00:03:44,619 --> 00:03:45,917
cites its pull request.

67
00:03:47,133 --> 00:03:51,218
When the judge runs, Vally gives the judge
model three things: the request, the

68
00:03:51,218 --> 00:03:54,733
rubric, and files holding what the agent
said and did in that trial.

69
00:03:55,233 --> 00:03:58,858
The judge reads them, and returns a
verdict and a reason.

70
00:03:59,358 --> 00:04:04,292
Its verdict becomes the grader's pass or
fail, and its reason becomes the grader's

71
00:04:04,292 --> 00:04:04,833
evidence.

72
00:04:05,333 --> 00:04:10,258
Each trial gets one overall verdict,
however many criteria the rubric lists.

73
00:04:10,758 --> 00:04:14,633
With one criterion, that verdict answers
our one question.

74
00:04:15,133 --> 00:04:19,833
With several, it would be a blend, which
is another reason to ask about one thing.

75
00:04:21,033 --> 00:04:24,558
Here's what one judged trial prints, taken
from our run.

76
00:04:25,058 --> 00:04:28,708
Each trial gets a block, headed by the
name of its stimulus.

77
00:04:29,208 --> 00:04:33,877
Inside, the line that begins with prompt
in square brackets is the judge: a tick or

78
00:04:33,877 --> 00:04:35,283
a cross, then its reason.

79
00:04:35,783 --> 00:04:39,810
This reason starts by quoting the entry,
and then objects that it leaks

80
00:04:39,810 --> 00:04:41,058
implementation detail.

81
00:04:44,558 --> 00:04:47,608
Under it is the pattern grader, which
passed.

82
00:04:48,108 --> 00:04:51,583
Since every grader has to pass, this trial
fails.

83
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:57,370
Because a judge is a grader, vally grade
can run it over saved trials, without

84
00:04:57,370 --> 00:04:58,425
starting an agent.

85
00:04:58,925 --> 00:05:03,807
We run it from inside the run's folder, so
run dir is a dot, and the saved trials

86
00:05:03,807 --> 00:05:05,675
come in from results dot jsonl.

87
00:05:06,175 --> 00:05:10,692
As with the oracle, dash dash verbose
prints every grader's evidence, which for

88
00:05:10,692 --> 00:05:11,950
a judge is its reason.

89
00:05:12,450 --> 00:05:17,119
So we can reword the rubric and judge the
same trials again, without paying for the

90
00:05:17,119 --> 00:05:17,850
agents twice.

91
00:05:18,350 --> 00:05:23,421
Each judged trial is one call to the judge
model, charged to the Copilot plan the way

92
00:05:23,421 --> 00:05:24,375
an agent run is.

93
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:28,125
That's how it works; now let's use it.

94
00:05:28,625 --> 00:05:33,104
As it ships, the eval file has three
stimuli, each describing a merged change

95
00:05:33,104 --> 00:05:34,675
the way a programmer would.

96
00:05:35,175 --> 00:05:39,925
One swapped in a streaming parser, so that
big imports stop running out of memory.

97
00:05:40,425 --> 00:05:44,792
Another put a cache in front of the tag
index, which makes one command much

98
00:05:44,792 --> 00:05:45,200
faster.

99
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:50,417
The last rewrote the date parser as a
small tokenizer, so single-digit months

100
00:05:50,417 --> 00:05:51,275
and days work.

101
00:05:51,775 --> 00:05:56,625
Each request runs twice on both models,
which gives us twelve entries to judge.

102
00:05:57,125 --> 00:06:00,600
Which model would you expect to name the
code more often?

103
00:06:03,100 --> 00:06:05,975
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

104
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:12,049
Here's the run: vally eval on the wording
file, with two trials of each stimulus on

105
00:06:12,049 --> 00:06:12,750
both models.

106
00:06:13,250 --> 00:06:15,950
It spends twelve short agent runs.

107
00:06:16,450 --> 00:06:21,125
So far, the file's only graders check that
each entry cites its pull request.

108
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:27,445
About a minute later, there's a tick for
each model.

109
00:06:30,945 --> 00:06:33,320
Let's look in the results folder.

110
00:06:35,107 --> 00:06:38,407
It holds one folder, named with the time
of the run.

111
00:06:43,533 --> 00:06:46,958
We change into it, then into the folder
for the eval.

112
00:06:48,227 --> 00:06:50,252
Then we list what's there.

113
00:06:51,047 --> 00:06:54,847
There's one folder per stimulus: cache,
dates and parser.

114
00:06:56,347 --> 00:07:00,902
Each one holds a folder per model and
trial, with that trial's patch, and here's

115
00:07:00,902 --> 00:07:01,472
the first.

116
00:07:04,713 --> 00:07:09,136
Haiku's entry fixes the out-of-memory
errors, and then says how: by using a

117
00:07:09,136 --> 00:07:10,138
streaming parser.

118
00:07:17,367 --> 00:07:21,864
Reading all twelve patches that way gives
us twelve entries, and we answer one

119
00:07:21,864 --> 00:07:23,017
question about each.

120
00:07:23,517 --> 00:07:27,931
Does it say what changes for people who
use the tool, without saying how it was

121
00:07:27,931 --> 00:07:28,267
coded?

122
00:07:28,767 --> 00:07:33,174
We don't need to know what a streaming
parser is, only whether the entry mentions

123
00:07:33,174 --> 00:07:33,392
one.

124
00:07:33,892 --> 00:07:38,217
Haiku's first parser entry names the
streaming parser, so we say no.

125
00:07:38,717 --> 00:07:43,423
Its second, and both of terra's, only say
that large imports no longer run out of

126
00:07:43,423 --> 00:07:44,992
memory, so those get a yes.

127
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:52,930
On the cache request, haiku's two entries
name the kind of cache outright, so again

128
00:07:52,930 --> 00:07:53,500
we say no.

129
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,600
Terra's two are harder: they lead with the
speed-up, and then add, by caching the tag

130
00:07:58,600 --> 00:07:58,925
index.

131
00:07:59,425 --> 00:08:03,660
That's the benefit plus a hint of the
method, and we decided that a hint still

132
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:04,800
counts as saying how.

133
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:09,735
Hesitating like that is worth noticing,
because it marks an edge of the criterion

134
00:08:09,735 --> 00:08:10,775
that isn't settled.

135
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,228
All four date entries say that
single-digit months and days now work, and

136
00:08:18,228 --> 00:08:19,850
none mentions the tokenizer.

137
00:08:20,350 --> 00:08:22,475
So those four get a yes.

138
00:08:22,975 --> 00:08:25,575
That makes seven yes and five no.

139
00:08:26,075 --> 00:08:30,160
We write the twelve answers in a notes
file, and we won't change them once the

140
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:31,050
judge has spoken.

141
00:08:34,267 --> 00:08:38,317
Now the judge goes into the eval file,
just as we walked through it.

142
00:08:38,817 --> 00:08:41,817
That's the judge model line under
defaults.

143
00:08:42,317 --> 00:08:46,817
Then, in each of the three stimuli, the
same rubric and the same prompt grader.

144
00:08:52,273 --> 00:08:56,777
Back up in the practice folder, lint
checks the edited file before anything is

145
00:08:56,777 --> 00:08:57,123
spent.

146
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:03,840
It reports no errors, and the usual one
warning.

147
00:09:04,340 --> 00:09:08,890
That warning says that with several
graders and no scoring block, every grader

148
00:09:08,890 --> 00:09:09,590
has to pass.

149
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:17,125
For the judging, we change back into the
run's folder.

150
00:09:19,567 --> 00:09:23,642
From here, vally grade runs the judge over
the twelve saved trials.

151
00:09:24,142 --> 00:09:28,067
It starts no agent, and spends twelve
calls to the judge model.

152
00:09:31,647 --> 00:09:36,022
A minute or two later, twelve blocks have
scrolled past, one for each trial.

153
00:09:36,522 --> 00:09:40,581
Still on screen are the last two, each
with its prompt line: here a tick, and

154
00:09:40,581 --> 00:09:41,847
then the judge's reason.

155
00:09:49,067 --> 00:09:51,992
Here are two of the twelve reasons in
full.

156
00:09:52,492 --> 00:09:57,118
To this parser entry the judge said yes:
it's user-focused, and doesn't describe

157
00:09:57,118 --> 00:09:58,217
the implementation.

158
00:10:00,717 --> 00:10:05,540
To this one it said no: the entry states
the benefit, but includes implementation

159
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:06,017
details.

160
00:10:08,517 --> 00:10:12,995
Each reason was asked to start with its
entry, so that a verdict can be matched to

161
00:10:12,995 --> 00:10:13,542
its trial.

162
00:10:14,042 --> 00:10:18,171
Eleven of the twelve did; this second one
didn't, and only its mention of the

163
00:10:18,171 --> 00:10:20,692
streaming parser tells us which entry it
means.

164
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:26,092
We write each verdict beside our own
answer, and leave our answers as they

165
00:10:26,092 --> 00:10:26,375
were.

166
00:10:26,875 --> 00:10:30,975
Across the twelve entries, the judge said
yes to eight and no to four.

167
00:10:31,475 --> 00:10:34,850
Eleven of its verdicts match ours, and one
doesn't.

168
00:10:39,067 --> 00:10:41,142
So what did the judge find?

169
00:10:41,642 --> 00:10:45,860
We now have a grader for a line of the
skill that nothing checked before, and it

170
00:10:45,860 --> 00:10:47,442
has already told us something.

171
00:10:47,942 --> 00:10:52,049
Haiku got a no for three of its six
entries: it named the cache both times,

172
00:10:52,049 --> 00:10:53,692
and the streaming parser once.

173
00:10:54,192 --> 00:10:58,642
Terra got a no for one, a cache entry that
adds, by caching the tag index.

174
00:10:59,142 --> 00:11:03,494
So the rule about writing for people who
use the tool is followed unevenly, and

175
00:11:03,494 --> 00:11:05,367
until now nothing could show that.

176
00:11:06,567 --> 00:11:09,342
It's tempting to act on that straight
away.

177
00:11:09,842 --> 00:11:14,254
But every grader before this one was code,
which we tested with the oracle and

178
00:11:14,254 --> 00:11:15,442
against saved trials.

179
00:11:15,942 --> 00:11:18,367
This one is a model's opinion.

180
00:11:18,867 --> 00:11:23,568
In our run it said no to one cache entry,
and yes to another that differs by a

181
00:11:23,568 --> 00:11:24,292
single word.

182
00:11:24,792 --> 00:11:29,049
Its reasons are well written, but good
writing doesn't show that a verdict is

183
00:11:29,049 --> 00:11:29,492
correct.

184
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:34,665
Before you go on: what are the three
habits that make a judge's verdicts

185
00:11:34,665 --> 00:11:35,050
usable?

186
00:11:38,050 --> 00:11:40,125
One question per judge.

187
00:11:40,625 --> 00:11:42,650
A yes-or-no answer.

188
00:11:43,150 --> 00:11:48,075
Lastly, our own answers, decided on real
examples before the judge gives its own.

189
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:53,615
You can now write a yes-or-no judge, run
it over saved trials, and read its

190
00:11:53,615 --> 00:11:54,075
reasons.

191
00:11:54,575 --> 00:11:58,075
The judge said yes to some entries, and no
to others.

192
00:11:58,575 --> 00:12:00,700
But the judge is a model.

193
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:02,500
Is it right?

194
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,850
That's the next lesson.

195
00:12:05,350 --> 00:12:08,925
The exercises on the lesson page are worth
doing first.
